welcome to CBS this morning we began  with some sad news it's a plane crash  and the cause of that plane crash that  killed 10 people near Dallas is still a  mystery at this hour one day after the  accident the private aircraft crashed  into a hangar and burst into flames  shortly after takeoff yesterday two crew  members and eight passengers were killed  this morning you can see a gaping hole  in that hangar the plane was supposed to  go to Florida  Mireya Vieira L is at the scene in  Addison Texas about 15 miles north of  Dallas Maria what else do we know about  the crash well we know right now is that  this small plane is often used for  business flights witnesses say they saw  the plane take off and then come  crashing down after it lost altitude  into the hangar the privately owned  hangar that you see behind me it looked  like that hangar might be two different  colors but that middle shiny area is  actually scorch marks left behind from  this accident massive flames and thick  black smoke poured from the hangar at  the Addison County Airport Sunday  morning everybody we had a ya accident  on the field  a small private airplane plunged into a  hangar at 9-11 a.m. killing all 10 on  board we know that there were two flight  crew members and that there were eight  passengers we don't have a lot of detail  beyond that the twin engine Beechcraft  was taken off for st. Petersburg Florida  witnesses say it lifted off the runway  reaching about 200 feet when it began  losing power and altitude the plane that  veered left and dropped its left wing  before crashing into the hangar the  airplane was damaged by impact forces  and a post-impact fire NTSB officials on  the ground are combing through debris  for any clues to what caused the crash  anytime there's a significant fire  situation in this case it does pose more  of a challenge for the investigators  aviation expert captain Laura ein  settler says officials will be  considering all possible causes  including engine failure pilot error or  weight issues it's ten passengers plus  fuel plus a lot of possible luggage  could also play a role in the aircraft  performance if they did say lose an  engine  or something on takeoff it's still  unclear who actually owns this  particular plane because there's a  little confusion with the tail number  right now what we do know is the  previous owner was a charter company out  of the Chicago area the NTSB could take  up to a full year for their full  investigation but a preliminary report  could be ready and as early as two weeks  Tony  yeah thank you very much
 

